# Southmeadow (Docs/Design/62-talk.md, section 3): grain fields and orchards in the far south. Cheerful; harvest,
# fruit, chaff and windfalls. Drafts (phase 3), to be written properly with you (phase 7).

grammar southmeadow
greet = Good harvest | Harvest to you
greet.broad = Good 'arvest
farewell = Ripe days | Good harvest
agree = Ripe and right | Aye, ripe
refuse = Not this season
surprise = Well, pick me | Bless the barley
annoyed = Blight take it | Wormy luck
very = full
good = ripe
bad = blighted | wormy
luck = a windfall
child = sprout
pet!! = my apple | my plum
hot = sun-ripe | baking
tired = harvested
nonsense = chaff
tag = , eh
oath! = Sheaf and sickle | Blight take it | Apples and ale
home = my own orchard
# (Phase 7, the rest of plain's words.)
address = friend | neighbour
address[other.stage=young] = sprout | young 'un
bed = my straw | my bed
cold = chilly | nippy
dear! = my plum | my sweet | love
drunk = cider-merry | ripe
hungry = starved | hollow
hurt = bruised | sore
little = a pip | a little
money = coin | penny-pieces
rain = shower | rain
robbery = Robbery, ripe as you like | Picked clean, like a windfall
saying! = What's ripe is ripe | There's always another harvest | The sun comes round again
thanks = Thank you kindly | Bless you
work = the harvest | the picking | the work
worthless = chaff | wormy
# (Phase 7, the conversations' words.)
wages = pay | harvest money
boss = the farmer | the master
busy = harvest-busy | run off my paws
slow = slow | lazy as July
cheated = robbed of the sheaf | done
mum! = Mam | Mother | Ma
dad! = Da | Pa | Father
gran = Granny | Gran
grandad = Grandad | Gaffer
supper = supper | harvest supper
toffs = the city quality | the fine folk
skint = skint | not a pip
posh = grand | fancy
busybody = chatterer | gossip
fool = you turnip | you great windfall
daft = daft as a scarecrow | daft
toast = Good harvest | Here's to the apple | Ripe days
drink = a cup of cider | a jar
wind = harvest wind | a breeze
ale = cider | ale
thief = orchard-robber | thief
grave = the green ground | the churchyard
grub = victuals | grub
tasty = ripe | lovely
snug = snug | sun-warm
beasts = the stock | the beasts
poorly = wilted | poorly
remedy = honey and apple vinegar | a nettle brew | camomile
olddays = the old harvests | days gone by
blessing = Good harvest to you | Ripe days | The sun keep you
bless = Sun keep you | Good harvest
someday = some fine harvest | one day
far = a long walk north | a fair step
stranger = northerner | stranger
